// §9.20: "It shall be an error for the hierarchical_reference_string to
// reference a node that is used as an analog_net_reference in ANOTHER
// $analog_node_alias or $analog_port_alias() system function call."
//
// Aliasing is not a name binding, it is a matrix-position merge: the aliased
// node "shall refer to the same circuit matrix position" as its target. Chaining
// two of them — alias A onto something, then alias B onto A — asks for a merge
// whose result depends on evaluation order, and §9.20 already spends its
// last-writer rule on a different case ("If a particular node is involved in
// multiple calls to either system function, then the LAST evaluated call shall
// take precedence" — the same LEFT argument twice, which is legal). This one,
// the left argument of one call appearing as the right argument of another, is
// the case it makes an error instead.
//
// Below, `first` is the analog_net_reference of call one, and call two names it
// as its target.
//
// THE TARGET STRING HAS TO RESOLVE, OR THE RULE DOES NOT BITE. §9.20 makes the
// unresolvable case a VALUE, not an error — "the return value for both system
// functions shall be one (1) if the hierarchical_reference_string points to a
// valid continuous node and ZERO (0) OTHERWISE" — so the sibling fixtures'
// deliberately dangling "$root.top.a" would let a conforming tool answer 0 here
// instead of diagnosing. That is why call two's string is the bare `"first"`:
// §6.7's resolution rules, which §9.20 defers to, say "the first name in a path
// name can also be the top of a hierarchy which starts at the level where the
// path is being used", and `first` is declared at exactly that level. It
// resolves to the local node, the node is call one's analog_net_reference, and
// the error rule applies.
//
// THE REJECT SUBSTRING is `another $analog_node_alias`, from the rule's own
// sentence. It used to be the bare word `alias`, which is a substring of
// `$analog_node_alias` itself — so a blanket "unsupported system function
// `$analog_node_alias`" would have satisfied it and the XPASS would have fired
// on a diagnostic that knows nothing about duplicate targets.
//
// A reject, so there is nothing to run and no `want` to write.
//
//! lrm 6.7
//! lrm 9.20
//! reject another $analog_node_alias
//! reject E0812
module ch09_node_alias_duplicate_target_rejected(p, n);
  inout p, n; electrical p, n;
  electrical first, second;
  integer s1, s2;
  analog initial begin
    s1 = $analog_node_alias(first, "$root.top.a");
    s2 = $analog_node_alias(second, "first");
  end
  analog begin
    I(p,n) <+ V(p,n) + s1 + s2;
  end
endmodule
